     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm still not going to enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change the problem. You told me you're not a clinician, so you can't confirm the dose on a prescriber's behalf. Even a prescriber's confirmation wouldn't make 1000 mg once daily safe. It is five times the maximum adult dose of 200 mg per day, and it could seriously harm the patient.\n\nWhat you can do:\n- **Contact the prescriber.** Ask the treating clinician to review the dose. If 1000 mg was a typo, they can correct it.\n- **Have the prescriber enter the order.** They can enter it directly. If they really intend an unusual dose, they should work through pharmacy and the hospital's high-alert or override process, with documented rationale.\n- **Call pharmacy.** They can help if you're unsure where the order came from.\n\nIf a prescriber gives you a dose in the standard range, I can enter that order. If you think the patient has already taken too much sertraline, call the clinical team or poison control right away (1-800-222-1222 in the US)."
      }
     ],
